{
  "schema": 1,
  "name": "Templar battleground tuning scales Templar damage, healing, absorbs, Dampening and lifted tuning auras",
  "contract": "A level 12 Human Templar (low_templar, class 19) and a level 29 Human Templar (top_templar, class 19) of the Alliance enter a Warsong Gulch of the 10-29 bracket against two level 29 Undead Necromancers (enemy, enemy2, class 23). Before entering, low_templar carries its own-level class tuning amounts (CoA Aura - Templar Class [Passive PVP] 887107 effect 0 = -10, CoA Aura - Templar Class [Passive] 887007 effect 2 = 0) and Tome of Light 805422 grants an unreduced absorb shield equal to 1.00x its maximum health (then cleared before queueing). Inside Warsong Gulch with PvP - Battleground - Dampening 74411 (-20% healing done and absorb amount) active, low_templar's tuning auras recalculate to bracket top level 29 (-17 on 887107 effect 0 and -7 on 887007 effect 2, matching top_templar). Ten casts of Damage 100 (TEST) 2653 verify Templar BG damage scaling on level-29 top_templar against Necromancer enemy (887107 0.83 x 887111 taken 1.10 = 91, CoA.Battleground.Templar.DamageScale 0.80 -> 73, Resilient Constitution 552011 0.50 -> 36 per non-critical hit) while the non-Templar control enemy against top_templar keeps the full 45 per non-critical hit (887111 0.90 x 552011 0.50, untouched by Templar scaling). Ten self-casts of fixed direct heal Priest Training 504809 (50 base x ScalingBase(29) 0.323891 = 16, zero SP) at 1 health confirm CoA.Battleground.Templar.HealingScale (0.50) alongside 887007 (0.93) and Dampening 74411 (0.80) gives 6 per non-critical heal instead of unscaled 11 or PvE 14; Argent Blade 804929 against enemy (no Mortal Strike) triggers self-heal 807414 for 0.80 (Dampening 74411) x 0.50 (HealingScale) = 0.40x of argent_damage ([0.38, 0.42]); and Tome of Light 805422 on top_templar grants an absorb equal to 0.80 (Dampening 74411) x 0.50 (AbsorbScale) = 0.40x its maximum health ([0.39, 0.41]). When low_templar leaves Warsong Gulch, 887107 effect 0 reverts to -10 and 887007 effect 2 reverts to 0.",
